## 1. PEAS Framework

| Component | Description |
|---|---|
| **Performance measure** | Safe, timely, fuel-efficient delivery of cargo; minimizing total distance/time traveled; avoiding collisions; obeying traffic laws; minimizing wear on trailer/cargo damage; customer satisfaction |
| **Environment** | Kenyan road network (highways, towns, junctions, traffic, pedestrians, other vehicles, weather, road conditions, roundabouts) |
| **Actuators** | Steering wheel/motor, accelerator, brakes, gear shift, trailer coupling control, indicators, headlights, horn |
| **Sensors** | GPS/navigation unit, cameras, LIDAR/RADAR, proximity/ultrasonic sensors, speedometer, trailer load sensors, digital road maps |

## 2. Environment Properties

The five environment definitions applied to the trailer agent:

| Property | Classification | Justification |
|---|---|---|
| Fully vs Partially observable | Partially observable | Sensors (camera/LIDAR range) don't reveal the entire road network at once — only the immediate surroundings |
| Deterministic vs Stochastic | Stochastic | Outcomes depend on unpredictable factors: other drivers, weather, road obstructions |
| Episodic vs Sequential | Sequential | Current driving decisions (speed, lane position) affect future states/decisions along the route |
| Static vs Dynamic | Dynamic | The environment changes while the agent deliberates (other vehicles move, traffic lights change) |
| Discrete vs Continuous | Continuous | Steering angle, speed, and position are continuous values, not discrete steps |

**Additional property:** the environment is also **multi-agent**, since other drivers, pedestrians and vehicles act independently in the same environment.

